ed)","SoilHealth: insufficient sourced data (6 of 20 trait values sourced)"],"why_suggested":"Your current rotation, used as the reference for every comparison.","evidence":[{"claim":"Share of the grain crops' critical periods falling in months the model found costly","data_source_id":"D2;D1;S01","metric":"stress exposure (model-weighted)","value":0.581},{"claim":"Share of the year with bare soil","data_source_id":"S01","metric":"bare fraction","value":0.566},{"claim":"Bare soil during months that bring Pocahontas County's heavy-rain days","data_source_id":"D2","metric":"erosion exposure (share of heavy-rain days on bare soil)","value":0.244}],"benefits":["Familiar markets, equipment and management.","Lowest labour of the three in ISU budgets (about 2.2-2.6 operator hours/acre per year; S10)."],"information_gaps":["Field-level soil, drainage and slope: the tool uses county-scale data only.","Local markets and prices (see ISU Ag Decision Maker crop budgets, S10).","Your equipment, labour and custom-hire options.","Yield response of crops the model does not cover (oats, red clover, cereal rye)."],"risks_needing_local_review":["Soil is bare about 57% of the year, including months with heavy rain.","A two-crop cycle gives soybean cyst nematode a host every other year (S11)."],"modeled_years":2,"not_modeled_years":0,"cycle_years":2,"confidence_note":"2 of 2 rotation years are modelled; 8 of 20 trait values sourced, 20 not yet verified by a human. Scores are relative and come from a transparent formula, not a predicted yield.","trait_coverage":{"sourced":8,"total":20,"verified_by_human":0,"unverified":20},"sources":["D1","D2","S01","S10"],"support_line":"Supports your decision; does not replace your judgment or local expert advice."},"B":{"strategy_id":"B","name":"Corn \u2192 soybean + winter cereal rye","criteria":{"StressExposure":0.0,"BareAndErosion":1.0,"IncomeStability":0.0,"LaborBurden":0.0,"WaterUse":null,"SoilHealth":null},"excluded_criteria":["WaterUse: insufficient sourced data (2 of 5 trait values sourced)","SoilHealth: insufficient sourced data (6 of 20 trait values sourced)"],"why_suggested":"The field is bare about 57% of the year under corn-soybean. Cereal rye seeded right after harvest (S05) covers the soil through winter and spring, when much of the county's heavy rain falls.","evidence":[{"claim":"Share of the grain crops' critical periods falling in months the model found costly","data_source_id":"D2;D1;S01","metric":"stress exposure (model-weighted)","value":0.581},{"claim":"Share of the year with bare soil","data_source_id":"S01;S04;S05","metric":"bare fraction","value":0.018},{"claim":"Bare soil during months that bring Pocahontas County's heavy-rain days","data_source_id":"D2","metric":"erosion exposure (share of heavy-rain days on bare soil)","value":0.023}],"benefits":["Bare-soil share falls from 57% to 2% of the year.","Rye produces 3,000-10,000 lb/acre of dry matter (SARE, S06) and holds soil and nutrients (S05).","Same cash crops, so no new grain markets are needed."],"information_gaps":["Field-level soil, drainage and slope: the tool uses county-scale data only.","Local markets and prices (see ISU Ag Decision Maker crop budgets, S10).","Your equipment, labour and custom-hire options.","Yield response of crops the model does not cover (oats, red clover, cereal rye)."],"risks_needing_local_review":["Rye decomposition ties up soil nitrogen for the next corn crop, more if rye has started jointing; terminate at least 10-14 days before corn planting (ISU, S04).","Possible corn yield drag after rye; Iowa survey data show smaller drag for experienced users (S12).","Seeding right after harvest and spring termination timing both depend on the season (S04; S05).","Without cost-share or grazing, cover crops returned about -$38 to -$49 per acre in an Iowa survey (S12); check local cost-share programs."],"modeled_years":2,"not_modeled_years":0,"cycle_years":2,"confidence_note":"2 of 2 rotation years are modelled; 13 o